Strict alignment: keep the aligned path of a checked access to the test and the access (applies after
perf-35, perf-39 and perf-67).

Under JitRiscV64StrictAlign an access whose address the JIT cannot prove aligned is emitted as

    addi  t, base, off
    andi  tmp, t, size-1
    bnez  tmp, slow
    ld    dst, 0(t)
    j     done
  slow:
    <byte-wise>
  done:

so the usual, aligned case runs five instructions for one load. Three changes cut it to three:

- The byte-wise sequence comes first and the test branches over it (beqz tmp, fast). On a zkVM a taken
  branch costs what an untaken one does, so the j now sits on the rare path. The alignment-checked
  unrolled block copy gets the same order.
- An address that is a base plus an offset that is a multiple of the access size stays an address mode:
  the base then has the alignment of the address, so the test reads the base, the wide access takes the
  offset in its immediate and so does every byte of the fallback (offset..offset+size-1 must fit a
  12-bit immediate). No addi.
- localloc space is STACK_ALIGN'ed (genLclHeap), so KnownAlignmentLog2 proves accesses through a
  stackalloc'd buffer and they lose the test altogether.

Nethermind stateless guest on SP1 (sp1-runner v6.6.0, master cd336c4ccc), cycles:
  block 26055159: 108,404,988 -> 102,844,417 (-5.13%), gas -1.44%
  block 26112793: 240,469,267 -> 226,298,346 (-5.89%), gas -1.86%
  geomean over 11 mainnet blocks: cycles -5.57%, gas -1.72%; outputs identical on all 11.
The JIT change alone, without the localloc proof, was -3.4% / -3.8%. Only builds with
JitNoUnalignedAccess change: the ZisK guest builds byte-identical.
Regression test: tools/tests/StrictAlignment/FastPath.

